1	//! The user's real jj configuration, instead of jj-lib's built-in defaults.
2	//!
3	//! **Why this exists.** Every entry point here used to build settings from
4	//! `StackedConfig::with_defaults()`, whose compiled-in `misc.toml` sets `user.name = ""`,
5	//! `user.email = ""` and `signing.backend = "none"`. That is not a cosmetic gap on the one code path
6	//! that *writes*:
7	//!
8	//! - `snapshot_working_copy` rewrites `@` whenever the working copy has moved, and jj-lib's
9	//!   `CommitBuilder::for_rewrite_from` unconditionally sets `commit.committer = settings.signature()`.
10	//!   With defaults-only settings that stamps an **empty committer name and email** onto the working
11	//!   copy commit — for every jj user, on every status refresh that finds a change. It is usually
12	//!   invisible (`jj log` shows the author) and self-heals on the next real `jj` command, but it does
13	//!   NOT heal if `@` is pushed as-is, which the app's own Push button does via `jj git push --change @`.
14	//! - `Signer::from_settings` reads `signing.backend` from the same settings, so `can_sign()` is false
15	//!   and `write_to_store` sets `secure_sig = None`. Under the default `behavior = "keep"` that means
16	//!   rewriting an already-signed `@` silently **drops its signature**.
17	//!
18	//! **jj-lib does no discovery.** It supplies the primitives (`ConfigLayer::load_from_file`,
19	//! `StackedConfig::add_layer`, the `ConfigSource` precedence order) and nothing else — locating the
20	//! files is jj-*cli*'s job, and jj-cli is not vendored. So the chain below is ours, and it mirrors
21	//! what `jj config path --user` reports.
22	//!
23	//! **Best-effort by design.** A layer that cannot be read or parsed is skipped rather than failing
24	//! the call. A malformed `~/.config/jj/config.toml` should cost the user their customisations, not
25	//! their Changes panel — degrading to jj's defaults is exactly what this module already did for
